# SFT execution status v0.120: reuse the working import path

2026-09-27. **Gate 5 remains incomplete, but a complete tiny CPU import and
packaging chain now passes.** The four new integration cases plus 84 focused
regressions passed on the existing Mac CPython 3.13.7 runtime: 88 tests,
zero failures/errors/skips. No dependency installation was needed.

## What was actually exercised

`generated DCP -> import_torch_v5 -> package_v2 -> actual SFT checksum consumer
-> native HF filename reload`

The test calls the real components without substituting their implementations.
It covers single-shard and multi-shard BF16 exports, exact tensor equality,
preservation of FP32 rotary buffers, tokenizer-file copying, before/after input
hashes, private file permissions, and a second read-only package verification.
The actual SFT checksum verifier is exercised both through the package's path
adapter and independently on an ordinary Path. Corrupt weights are rejected
by both consumers; mismatched tokenizer content cannot produce a completed
receipt or manifest.

The tokenizer files are synthetic opaque bytes, not a usable tokenizer. This
is not a tokenization, training, real-4B, Linux-containment, or performance test.
Forward passes, optimizer steps and network connects were forbidden. The
final regression run also denied access to real payload directories and
subprocess launches; it observed no prohibited attempts.

The first new test setup incorrectly reused a Mock budget callback returning
a Mock rather than None. It was corrected to a real monotonic deadline check.
That was a test setup error, not an actual checkpoint import attempt. No
production conversion or packaging defect was found in this integration.

## Selected preparation route; not a live execution authorization

The user asked to resume with the best approach after discussing the cost of
continuing the per-write broker. Retain the existing Lumi architecture,
pretrained checkpoint binding, tokenizer, curated data, masking, checksums,
save/resume logic and tested import/package code. Do not restart those jobs.

Prepare a **separate isolated-Linux execution route**. Do not extend the
unfinished arbitrary-native-write broker/controller/guardian protocol as a
prerequisite for first SFT. Its code and evidence remain intact; its previous
failures have not been relabelled as passes.

This revises the intended execution protection, not the model or data:

| Concern | Simplified route requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Host | Dedicated Linux VM, never the user's already-busy Lambda instance |
| Inputs | Exact authenticated checkpoint/tokenizer/source bindings; no writable aliases available to the worker; read-only mounts and before/after hashes |
| Worker | Unprivileged, no acquisition credentials, no network, no GPU for import |
| Resources | Independently enforced RAM, swap, process, disk-capacity and wall-time limits; bounded logs/temp files; whole-workload termination |
| Content accounting | Retain existing cooperative DCP/HF/package read/write counters and caps |
| Native writes | Do not claim that filesystem capacity bounds cumulative native bytes written |
| Attempts | Fresh exclusive attempt; preserve failures; no retry after start |
| Billing | Named host, agreed duration/rate/cost limit and lifecycle action; stopping a worker alone does not stop instance billing |

The old host contract requires cumulative native-write enforcement. **It is
not satisfied by this alternative.** Its v0.1 file and every old execution
authorization remain unchanged. The revised route must have its own explicit
host-bound admission; no old approval flag or private entry is repurposed to
bypass the old contract. No live authorization or host admission is created
by this document. Production eligibility remains false.

The earlier 64-GiB worker-memory / 128-GiB host-storage / 3,600-second import
proposal is a planning reference, not a measured requirement or approved
host. Final host settings must include supervisor, runtime, logs and staging
headroom. A disk-capacity budget is separate from the retained cooperative
write budgets. H100 capacity or GPU count is not inferred from this tiny test.

## Remaining work, in execution order

1. **Local integration:** connect one standard Linux supervisor and entry to
   the existing CPU coordinator and package verifier, under the revised
   host contract. Test the same entry on generated input, including timeout,
   memory/storage exhaustion and cleanup. Do not build a new conversion
   kernel, package format or generic syscall broker. Public/private old
   execution entries stay closed; the new route is not implemented yet.
2. **Target check and Gate-5 completion:** select/bind the dedicated host,
   runtime and finite resource/cost limits; run the generated-input native
   check there. Only after that succeeds and real-input acquisition/import
   is admitted, use the bound S3 base checkpoint, perform the real CPU import,
   and independently verify the complete SFT package. No training GPU is
   needed for the import itself. This turn did not access S3.
3. **Gate 6:** fix/bind SFT-specific launch approval before data/CUDA setup,
   then run the bounded H100 smoke with save/resume and measure memory before
   choosing full-run geometry. Train and seal only after that acceptance.
   The existing `train_distributed_sft_v2.py` initializes distributed/CUDA
   before the worker's generic authorization check; it is not a ready-to-run
   approved launcher. Do not bypass that check or start it unchanged.
4. **Gates 7 and 8:** frozen quality/safety evaluation, then serving/deployment
   acceptance. Private research artifacts do not grant production rights.

These are remaining outcomes, not four small tests or a promised day count.
The next action is still local supervisor/entry integration, **not GPU rental**.
The original eight-gate roadmap remains; only the Gate-5 execution approach
is being simplified. Older roadmap/status snapshots are historical.

## Reproduction and records

Run only in the already installed isolated ML runtime; no install or download:

```sh
PYTHONPATH=src PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 \
TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1 HF_HUB_DISABLE_TELEMETRY=1 \
HF_HUB_DISABLE_PROGRESS_BARS=1 TOKENIZERS_PARALLELISM=false \
CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 MKL_NUM_THREADS=1 \
artifacts/lumi-sft-import-runtime-v0.1/venv/bin/python -B -m unittest \
  tests.test_sft_checkpoint_import_integration_v2 \
  tests.test_sft_checkpoint_import_torch_v5 \
  tests.test_sft_checkpoint_import_torch_v5_source \
  tests.test_sft_checkpoint_package_v2
```

The additional outer audit-hook/120-second alarm used in the recorded run is
described in the verification record; the command above runs the same suite,
not an OS-isolation qualification. Ruff also passed for the new test file.

- Tests: `tests/test_sft_checkpoint_import_integration_v2.py`.
- Aggregate result and source pins:
  `evidence/lumi-4b-sft-import-chain-integration-v0.1.json`.
- Preserved predecessor: `docs/sft-execution-status-v0.119.md`.

No real model/tokenizer/dataset reads, inference, training, remote/instance
actions, publication, staging, commit or push occurred. No prior source or
evidence file was edited. Existing untracked work remains in place.
